,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8]],"output":[[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8],[8,4,4,7,8,8,8,7,1,7,8,8,8,7,4,4,8],[8,4,7,7,8,8,8,7,7,7,8,8,8,7,7,4,8],[8,7,7,3,8,8,8,7,7,7,8,8,8,3,7,7,8],[8,8,8,8,3,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,3,8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,7,7,4,7,7,7,4,7,7,8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,7,4,4,7,1,7,4,4,7,8,8,8,8],[8,7,7,7,7,7,7,4,1,4,7,7,7,7,7,7,8],[8,1,7,7,7,7,1,1,7,1,1,7,7,7,7,1,8],[8,7,7,7,7,7,7,4,1,4,7,7,7,7,7,7,8],[8,8,8,8,7,4,4,7,1,7,4,4,7,8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,7,7,4,7,7,7,4,7,7,8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,3,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,3,8,8,8,8],[8,7,7,3,8,8,8,7,7,7,8,8,8,3,7,7,8],[8,4,7,7,8,8,8,7,7,7,8,8,8,7,7,4,8],[8,4,4,7,8,8,8,7,1,7,8,8,8,7,4,4,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"## How the puzzle works\n\n**What the input is made of.** Each input holds one 9×9 square pattern on a background of 8. Its central 3×3 is empty (background). So the square is really a 3×3 arrangement of 3×3 tiles: eight patterned tiles around one empty tile.\n\n**What the output adds, from the train pairs.**\n\n1. **The eight outer tiles reflect outward.** Each one is mirrored across its outer edge and placed in the matching spot one tile further out.\n   - In pair 1 the top‑middle input tile is `4 6 4 / 6 4 6 / 6 9 6`. The tile above it in the output is the same rows in reverse order, `6 9 6 / 6 4 6 / 4 6 4`. That is a vertical flip.\n   - The left‑middle tile is flipped horizontally and placed to its left.\n   - Corner tiles are flipped both ways (rotated 180°) and placed diagonally outward.\n   - Each copy therefore lies three cells beyond the square, at the matching edge or corner of the frame. That gives a 15×15 cross/star figure. Copies that would fall outside the grid are clipped.\n\n2. **The empty centre gets filled.** The fill comes from the 5×5 ring that touches the hole.\n   - In pair 3 that ring's top row is `3 5 2 5 3`.\n   - The centre becomes `3 2 3 / 2 5 2 / 3 2 3`. Its corners take the ring's corner colour (3) and its edges take the ring's mid‑edge colour (2). Its middle cell takes the colour next to the ring corner (5).\n   - Pair 1 checks out: ring row `9 6 9 6 9` gives `9 9 9 / 9 6 9 / 9 9 9`.\n   - Pair 2 checks out: ring row `7 1 7 1 7` gives `7 7 7 / 7 1 7 / 7 7 7`.\n   - In general, centre cell (i, j) is ring cell (2i, 2j), except the middle cell, which uses ring cell (0, 1).\n\n**Applying it to the test.** The test square spans rows and columns 4–12.\n- Its eight tiles get mirrored outward. The grid is 17×17, so the copies fit exactly, reaching rows/cols 1–15.\n- Its ring (rows/cols 6–10) has top row `4 7 1 7 4`. So the centre becomes `4 1 4 / 1 7 1 / 4 1 4`.\n\n**Construction stages for emit.** One stage per mirrored tile, then the centre fill, then the final canvas.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    ys, xs = np.where(g != bg)\n    y0, x0 = ys.min(), xs.min()\n    out = g.copy()\n    for by in range(3):\n        for bx in range(3):\n            if by == 1 and bx == 1:\n                continue\n            tile = g[y0 + 3*by:y0 + 3*by + 3, x0 + 3*bx:x0 + 3*bx + 3]\n            dy, dx = by - 1, bx - 1\n            t = tile\n            if dy != 0:\n                t = t[::-1, :]\n            if dx != 0:\n                t = t[:, ::-1]\n            ty = y0 + 3*by + 3*dy\n            tx = x0 + 3*bx + 3*dx\n            for i in range(3):\n                for j in range(3):\n                    yy, xx = ty + i, tx + j\n                    if 0 <= yy < H and 0 <= xx < W:\n                        out[yy, xx] = t[i, j]\n            emit(out, f\"Mirror tile ({by},{bx}) outward across its outer edge and stamp it beyond the frame.\")\n    ring = g[y0 + 2:y0 + 7, x0 + 2:x0 + 7]\n    for i in range(3):\n        for j in range(3):\n            if i == 1 and j == 1:\n                v = ring[0, 1]\n            else:\n                v = ring[2*i, 2*j]\n            out[y0 + 3 + i, x0 + 3 + j] = v\n    emit(out, \"Fill the central hole: corners/edges from the ring's corner/mid-edge colours, centre from the colour beside the ring corner.\")\n    emit(out, \"Final grid.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"rigid":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**The eight outer tiles reflect outward.** Each one is mirrored across its outer edge and placed in the matching spot one tile further out."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- Corner tiles are flipped both ways (rotated 180°) and placed diagonally outward."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- Its eight tiles get mirrored outward."}],"repeat":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"So the square is really a 3×3 arrangement of 3×3 tiles: eight patterned tiles around one empty tile."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"s reflect outward.** Each one is mirrored across its outer edge and placed in the matching spot one tile further out."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- In pair 1 the top‑middle input tile is `4 6 4 / 6 4 6 / 6 9 6`."}],"template":[{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Mirror tile (0,0) outward across its outer edge and stamp it beyond the frame."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Mirror tile (0,1) outward across its outer edge and stamp it beyond the frame."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Mirror tile (0,2) outward across its outer edge and stamp it beyond the frame."}],"topology":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**The empty centre gets filled.** The fill comes from the 5×5 ring that touches the hole."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Fill the central hole: corners/edges from the ring's corner/mid-edge colours, centre from the colour beside the ring corner."}],"dynamics":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Copies that would fall outside the grid are clipped."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"971d8da87